<Docs
  title="Philosophy"
  eyebrow="Respect · Tradition · Practice"
  description="SKN exists to support students between lessons, never to replace the dojo, the sensei, or the lineage."
  active="philosophy"
>
  <h2>Traditional learning first</h2>
  <p>
    Kata is not a diagram. It is breath, timing, posture, and intention, taught
    through demonstration, correction, and years of practice. Spatial Kata
    Notation cannot carry that. It only carries the skeleton: where you face,
    how you step, what technique is named, and what you want to remember.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Rule of deference:</strong> If your sensei’s teaching and an SKN
      sheet disagree, the sensei is correct. Update the sheet.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What this notation is for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Remembering
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embusen">embusen</a> after class
    </li>
    <li>Reviewing sequence before training alone</li>
    <li>Sharing a draft with training partners for discussion</li>
    <li>Annotating one possible bunkai reading beside a move</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this notation is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A replacement for dojo instruction</li>
    <li>A claim of style orthodoxy across ryuha</li>
    <li>A grading tool or “correct form” certificate</li>
    <li>A substitute for bunkai taught by your teacher</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Humility in writing</h2>
  <p>
    Every sheet should name the style or school reading it follows
    (for example <em>Shito-Ryu</em>, <em>dojo-specific</em>). Kata names travel
    widely; details do not. Write the version you were taught, and leave room
    for correction.
  </p>

  <h2>Practice more, argue less</h2>
  <p>
    The goal is simple: spend less time reconstructing the map of the kata, and
    more time polishing the kata itself. Notation serves memory. The floor
    serves mastery. The sensei serves transmission.
  </p>
</Docs>
